    ```yaml
    spec:
  grid: 10x10
  colors: water is 1; use 2-3 other colors for solid structures
  basin_walls:
    count: 2-3 bottom-anchored structures
    shape: adjacent lower basins/grooves with varied walls: U-like, L-like, staircase, or single vertical edge
    style: prefer organic variation; neighboring basins and U arms may have varying heights
    relation: neighboring basins are arranged so water can pass or overflow between them, not as isolated independent cups
    base: combined bottom bases cover most but not all columns, leaving some edge background
  guide:
    count: 0 or 1
    shape: optional upper guide/ramp entering from a left or right grid edge; each arm is 4-connected
    variants: one sloped arm or a two-arm bottom-open funnel
    role: directs a small supported source-water run into a target basin so it can settle or overflow onward
  water:
    color: 1
    count: guide samples usually have 3-10 water pixels; no-guide samples usually have 10-25
    input: every water pixel is supported from below; no water pixel has bg on both horizontal sides
    source_shapes: guide samples use small guide-adjacent runs; no-guide samples use larger side reservoirs or ragged sheets packed against an edge or wall
    stable_carryover: input may optionally retain a small portion of already-stable basin water while all the other source water moves
  overflow:
    pattern: water is poured into one primary basin/reservoir until it fills enough to spill into a neighbor
    frequency: ~50% examples should visibly use overflow between neighboring basins
    levels: final neighboring water levels may be equal or unequal, both as natural results of overflow
  transformation:
    rule: local water simulation
    details: water falls through bg; if blocked, it may move diagonally only through an open side cell; when supported, it slides along the row toward a unique reachable drop
    invariant: structure pixels unchanged and water count conserved
    output: water ends in stable basin cells; output water has non-bg or grid edge to left, below, and right

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first
  steps:
    - Draw bottom-anchored basin walls with varied shapes and colors.
    - Optionally add an open edge-entering guide aimed toward the basin area.
    - Get output by forward-pouring water one pixel at a time from the guide-adjacent source area or from a side reservoir, often filling one basin first and letting it overflow to a neighbor. (Implement each pour step by inserting one water pixel into a source cell and calling the solver as `main(grid, check_input=False, check_output=False)` rather than reimplementing the water physics.)
    - Reverse-move some output water back to a supported input source, then validate by running the solver forward.
    - Sample both overflow and non-overflow cases; overflow should be a core pattern, not an accidental side effect.
